L000530	\section{Time constant}
L000531	
L000532	Here, we derive Eq \ref{eq:T_equilibration} for $\mathcal{T}$, the time constant for relaxation into a new equilibrium state after an incremental jump in the chemical potential of $\sf A$.
L000533	
L000534	In keeping with our constitutive assumptions, we consider fast diffusion and, hence, homogeneous solute fraction $x^{\sf B}$ throughout the bulk of ${\sf B}$ at all times, $t$. Then,
L000535	
L000536	\be
L000537	\label{eq:SI_dxdt}
L000538	\frac{\dd x^{\sf B}}{\dd t} = J^{\sf B} \frac{A_{\rm V}}{\rho_0}
L000539	\ee
L000540	
L000541	with $A_{\rm V}$ the volume-specific surface area and $\rho_0$ the bulk density of sites for interstitials. Furthermore, we have
L000542	
L000543	\be
L000544	\label{eq:SI_djdt}
L000545	\frac{\dd J^{\sf B}}{\dd t}
L000546	=
L000547	 \frac{\dd x^{\sf B}}{\dd t} \,
L000548	 \frac{\dd J^{\sf B}}{\dd x^{\sf B}} \bigg \rvert_{\mu^{\sf A}_{\rm S} = \rm{const}, \, \mu^{\sf B}_{\rm S} = \mu^{\sf A}_{\rm S}} \,.
L000549	 \ee
L000550	
L000551	Analogously to the derivation of Eq \ref{eq:J_mu}, the derivative $J_x = \dd J^{\sf B}/\dd x^{\sf B}$ in Eq \ref{eq:SI_djdt} can again be based on Eq \ref{eq:W_total_closed_explicit} with Eq \ref{eq:transfer_coefficient_generic} for $\epsilon^{\sf T}$. The $\mu_{\rm S}$-derivative of $\epsilon^{\sf T}$ drops out, and one obtains
L000552	
L000553	\be
L000554	\begin{split}
L000555	\label{eq:SI_djdx}
L000556	J_x
L000557	&=
L000558	\frac{\dd J^{\sf B}}{\dd x^{\sf B}} \bigg \rvert_{\mu^{\sf A}_{\rm S} = \rm{const}, \, \mu^{\sf B}_{\rm S} = \mu^{\sf A}_{\rm S}} \\
L000559	&=
L000560	\frac{\dd J^{\sf B}}{\dd \mu^{\sf B}_{\rm S}} \bigg \rvert_{\mu^{\sf A}_{\rm S} = \rm{const}, \, \mu^{\sf B}_{\rm S} = \mu^{\sf A}_{\rm S}}  \,
L000561	\frac{\dd \mu^{\sf B}_{\rm S}}{\dd x^{\sf B}}\\
L000562	&=
L000563	-\frac{\dd J^{\sf B}}{\dd \mu^{\sf A}_{\rm S}}
L000564	\bigg \rvert_{\mu^{\sf B}_{\rm S} = \rm{const}, \,
L000565	\mu^{\sf A}_{\rm S} = \mu^{\sf B}_{\rm S}}   \,
L000566	\frac{\dd \mu^{\sf B}_{\rm S}}{\dd x^{\sf B}} \\
L000567	&=
L000568	-\frac{
L000569	J^{\rm eq}_{\mu}}{\chi^{\sf B}_{\rm S}} \,,
L000570	\end{split}
L000571	\ee
L000572	
L000573	where $\chi^{\sf B}_{\rm S}$ is the solute susceptibility of Eq \ref{eq:chi_definition} and the flux resistance parameter $J^{\rm eq}_{\mu}$ is defined (see main text) as
L000574	
L000575	\be
L000576	\label{eq:SI_J_mu}
L000577	J^{\rm eq}_{\mu}
L000578	=
L000579	\frac{\dd J^{\sf B}}{\dd \mu^{\sf A}_{\rm S}} \bigg \rvert_{\mu^{\sf B}_{\rm S} = \rm{const}, \, \mu^{\sf A}_{\rm S} = \mu^{\sf B}_{\rm S}}  \,.
L000580	\ee
L000581	
L000582	Note that the third line of Eq \ref{eq:SI_djdx} replaces the $\dd J^{\sf B}/\dd \mu^{\sf B}_{\rm S}$ derivative with $J^{\rm eq}_{\mu}$ of Eq \ref{eq:SI_J_mu}. In view of Eq 15, first changing the variable of differentiation from $\mu^{\sf B}_{\rm S}$ to $\mu^{\sf A}_{\rm S}$ and then accounting for $\mu^{\sf B}_{\rm S} = \mu^{\sf A}_{\rm S}$ at equilibrium leaves the magnitude of the derivative invariant but inverts its sign.
L000583	
L000584	Since our attention is here on small jumps, $\delta \mu_{\rm S}$, in $\mu_{\rm  S}$ around its current value, all quantities in Eqs \ref{eq:SI_dxdt} - \ref{eq:SI_djdx} -- and, hence, $J_x$ -- may be taken as constants when studying the response to the jump.
L000585	
L000586	Combining the above expressions, one obtains the differential equation
L000587	
L000588	\be
L000589	\label{eq:SI_J_vs_t_DEQ}
L000590	\frac{1}{J^{\sf B}}
L000591	\frac{\dd J^{\sf B}}{\dd t}
L000592	=
L000593	\frac{A_{\rm V}}{\rho_0} J_x \,,
L000594	\ee
L000595	
L000596	which is readily integrated, yielding
L000597	
L000598	\begin{gather}
L000599	
L000600	\nonumber
L000601	J^{\sf B}
L000602	=
L000603	J^{\sf B}_{t = 0}  \exp - \frac{t}{\mathcal T}
L000604	\\
L000605	
L000606	\nonumber
L000607	{\mathcal T}^{-1}
L000608	=
L000609	- \frac{A_{\rm V}}{\rho_0} J_x
L000610	=
L000611	+\frac{A_{\rm V}}{\rho_0} \, \frac{J^{\rm eq}_{\mu}}{\chi^{\sf B}_{\rm S}} \,,
L000612	\end{gather}
L000613	
L000614	which are Eqs \ref{eq:J_equilibration} and \ref{eq:T_equilibration} of the main text. ${\mathcal T}$ is the characteristic time for equilibration and $J^{\sf B}_0$ a constant (independent of $t$) prefactor. With the definition of $J^{\rm eq}_{\mu}$ in Eq \ref{eq:SI_J_mu}, the current at $t = 0$ is $J^{\sf B}_{t = 0}$
L000615	
L000616	\be
L000617	J^{\sf B}_{t = 0}
L000618	=
L000619	J^{\rm eq}_{\mu} \, \delta \mu_{\rm S} \,.
L000620	\ee
